== Chapter 8

#align(center)[THE CREDIBILITY OF SCRIPTURE SUFFICIENTLY PROVED INSOFAR AS NATURAL REASON ADMITS.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Beveridge’s summary.] This chapter has four parts. The first contains certain general proofs which may easily be gathered from the writings of both the Old and New Testament, namely, the arrangement of the sacred volume, its dignity, truth, simplicity, efficacy, and majesty, sec. 1, 2. The second part contains special proofs taken from the Old Testament, namely, the antiquity of the books of Moses, their authority, his miracles and prophecies, sec. 3-7; also the predictions of the other prophets and their wonderful harmony, sec. 8. A refutation of two objections to the books of Moses and the Prophets is added, sec. 9, 10. The third part sets out proofs gathered from the New Testament, for example, the harmony of the Evangelists in their account of heavenly mysteries, the majesty of the writings of John, Peter, and Paul, the remarkable calling of the Apostles and the conversion of Paul, sec. 11. The last part sets out the proofs drawn from church history: the unbroken consent of the Church in receiving and preserving divine truth, the invincible force of the truth in defending itself, the agreement of the godly (though otherwise differing so much from one another), the pious profession of the same doctrine by many illustrious men; and finally, the more than human constancy of the martyrs, sec. 12, 13. This is followed by a conclusion of the particular topic discussed.]

