1\. #smallcaps[It] would be in vain to fortify the authority of Scripture by argument, or to support it by the consent of the Church, or to confirm it by any other helps, if this certainty, higher and stronger than any human judgment can give, were absent. Until this better foundation has been laid, the authority of Scripture remains in suspense. On the other hand, once we have recognized that it is exempt from the common rule, and have received it reverently and as its dignity deserves, those proofs which were not strong enough to produce and fix a full conviction in our minds become most fitting helps. For it is remarkable how much we are confirmed in our belief when we consider more attentively how admirably the plan of divine wisdom contained in it is arranged—how perfectly free its doctrine is from everything that savors of earth—how beautifully it harmonizes in all its parts—and how rich it is in all the other qualities that give majesty to writing. Our hearts are still more firmly assured when we reflect that it is the dignity of the matter, more than the graces of style, that moves us to wonder at it. For it was by a remarkable arrangement of Providence that the sublime mysteries of the kingdom of heaven have for the most part been delivered in lowly and contemptible words. If they had been adorned with more splendid eloquence, the wicked might have quibbled that this was their whole force. But now, when an unpolished simplicity, almost bordering on crudeness, stirs a deeper reverence for itself than the loftiest flights of oratory, what does it show, if not that the Holy Scriptures are too mighty in the power of truth to need the rhetorician’s art?

So there was good reason for the Apostle’s declaration that the faith of the Corinthians was founded not on “men’s wisdom,” but on “God’s power,” (1 Cor 2:5), since his message and preaching among them had been “not with persuasive words of human wisdom, but with a demonstration of the Spirit’s power,” (1 Cor 2:4). For the truth is cleared of every doubt when, without support from outside, it is sufficient in itself alone. How specially this quality belongs to Scripture appears from this: no human writings, however skillfully composed, are at all able to affect us in a similar way. Read Demosthenes or Cicero, read Plato, Aristotle, or any others of that company: you will, I admit, feel wonderfully drawn, pleased, moved, enchanted. But turn from them to the reading of the Sacred Volume, and whether you want to or not, it will so affect you, so pierce your heart, so work its way into your very marrow, that, compared with the impression it makes, that of orators and philosophers will almost disappear. This makes it plain that in the Sacred Volume there is a truth divine, a something that makes it immeasurably superior to all the gifts and graces attainable by man.

2\. I admit, however, that in elegance and beauty, and even splendor, the style of some of the prophets is not surpassed by the eloquence of pagan writers. By examples of this kind the Holy Spirit was pleased to show that it was not for lack of eloquence that he used a rough and plain style in other places. But whether you read David, Isaiah, and others like them, whose speech flows sweet and pleasant, or Amos the herdsman, Jeremiah, and Zechariah, whose rougher idiom has the flavor of the countryside, that majesty of the Spirit which I mentioned is plain to see in all. I am well aware that, as Satan often apes God, so that by a false resemblance he may more easily worm his way into the minds of the simple, so he craftily spread the impious errors with which he deceived miserable men in an uncouth and half-barbarous style, and often used obsolete forms of expression in order to cloak his impostures. Anyone with a moderate share of sense needs no telling how empty and vile such affectation is. But as for the Holy Scriptures, however insolent men may try to carp at them, they are full of thoughts which it is clear that man could never have conceived. Let each of the prophets be examined, and not one will be found who does not rise far above human reach. Those who find their works insipid must be utterly without taste.

3\. As this subject has been treated at length by others, it will be enough here merely to touch on its main points. Besides the qualities already mentioned, great weight is due to the antiquity of Scripture.#footnote[Euseb. Prepar. Evang. lib. 2 c. 1. (Beveridge’s note.)] Whatever fables Greek writers may tell about the Egyptian theology, no monument of any religion exists which is not long later than the age of Moses. But Moses does not introduce a new Deity. He only sets forth the doctrine about the eternal God which the Israelites had received by tradition from their fathers, by whom it had been passed down, as it were, from hand to hand through a long series of ages. For what else does he do than lead them back to the covenant that had been made with Abraham? If he had brought them something they had never heard of, he could never have succeeded; but the talk of their deliverance from the bondage in which they were then held must have been familiar and well known to all, so that the very mention of it might at once raise the spirits of all. It is probable, moreover, that they had been informed of the number of four hundred years. Now if Moses (who is so much earlier than all other writers) traces the tradition of his doctrine back to so remote a period, it is obvious how far the Holy Scriptures must surpass all other writings in antiquity.

4\. Some perhaps may choose to believe the Egyptians, who carry their antiquity back to a period six thousand years before the world was created. But their idle talk, which even every pagan author has always held up to ridicule, I need not trouble to refute. Josephus, however, in his work against Apion, produces important passages from very ancient writers, from which we may gather that the doctrine delivered in the law was celebrated among all nations from the remotest ages, though it was neither read nor accurately known. And then, so that the malicious might have no ground for suspicion, and the ungodly no handle for quibbling, God has provided against both dangers in the most effectual way. When Moses relates the words which Jacob, under divine inspiration, spoke about his descendants almost three hundred years before, how does he ennoble his own tribe? He brands it with eternal disgrace in the person of Levi. “Simeon and Levi,” he says, “are brothers; their swords are weapons of violence. May I never enter their council; may I never join their assembly,” (Gen 49:5, 6). He could certainly have passed over this stigma in silence, not only to spare his own ancestor, but also to save both himself and his whole family from a share of the disgrace. How can any suspicion attach to a man who, by freely proclaiming that the first founder of his family was declared detestable by a divine oracle, neither looks after his own private interest nor shrinks from incurring ill will among his tribe, who must have been offended by his statement of the fact? Again, when he relates the wicked murmuring of his brother Aaron and his sister Miriam (Num 12:1), shall we say that he spoke from his own natural feelings, or that he obeyed the command of the Holy Spirit? Moreover, when he held supreme authority, why does he not give the office of High Priest to his sons, instead of consigning them to the lowest place? I only touch on a few points out of many; but the Law itself everywhere contains numerous proofs which fully vindicate the credibility of Moses, and put it beyond dispute that he comes forth, as it were, an angel of God from heaven.

5\. The many striking miracles which Moses relates are so many confirmations of the law he delivered and the doctrine he set forth.#footnote[Exod 24:18; Exod 34:29; Exod 19:16; Exod 40:34 Num 16:24; Num 20:10; Num 11:9] His being carried up into the mountain in a cloud; his remaining there forty days cut off from human company; his face shining during the giving of the law, as with the brightness of the noonday sun; the lightning that flashed on every side; the voices and thunder that echoed through the air; the blast of the trumpet blown by no human mouth; his entering the tabernacle while a cloud hid him from the people’s view; the miraculous vindication of his authority by the fearful destruction of Korah, Dathan, and Abiram, and all their impious faction; the stream instantly gushing from the rock when struck with his rod; the manna which rained from heaven at his prayer—did not God by all these proclaim aloud that he was an undoubted prophet? If anyone objects that I am taking disputed points for granted, the quibble is easily answered. Moses published all these things in the assembly of the people. How, then, could he possibly deceive the very eyewitnesses of what was done? Is it conceivable that he would have come forward and, while accusing the people of unbelief, obstinacy, ingratitude, and other crimes, have boasted that his doctrine had been confirmed in their own presence by miracles which they never saw?

6\. For it is also worth noting that the miracles which he relates are joined with unpleasant circumstances, which must have provoked opposition from the whole body of the people if there had been the smallest ground for it. So it is obvious that they were brought to assent only because they had already been convinced by their own experience. But because the fact was too clear for pagan writers to be free to deny that Moses did perform miracles, the father of lies suggested a slander, and ascribed them to magic.#footnote[Exod 9:11.] But how likely is a charge of magic against a man who held it in such horror that he ordered everyone who should merely consult soothsayers and magicians to be stoned? (Lev 20:27). Certainly no impostor deals in tricks without trying to raise his reputation by amazing the common people. But what does Moses do? By crying out that he and his brother Aaron are nothing (Exod 16:7), and that they merely carry out what God has commanded, he clears himself of every hint of suspicion. Again, if the facts are considered in themselves, what kind of spell could make manna rain from heaven every day, in quantity enough to feed a people, while anyone who gathered more than the appointed measure saw his unbelief punished by God when the manna turned to worms? To this we may add that God then allowed his servant to be subjected to so many severe trials that the ungodly cannot now gain anything by their clamor. When (as often happened) the people proudly and insolently rose up against him, when individuals conspired and attempted to overthrow him, how could any impostures have enabled him to escape their rage? The outcome plainly shows that by these means his doctrine was attested to all succeeding ages.

7\. Moreover, it is impossible to deny that he was guided by a prophetic spirit in assigning the sovereignty to the tribe of Judah in the person of Jacob (Gen 49:10), especially if we consider the fact itself, as the outcome has explained it. Suppose that Moses invented the prophecy; still, after he committed it to writing, four hundred years pass in which no mention is made of a scepter in the tribe of Judah. After Saul is anointed, the kingship seems fixed in the tribe of Benjamin (1 Sam 11:15). When David is anointed by Samuel (1 Sam 16:13), what visible reason is there for the transfer? Who could have looked for a king from the humble family of a herdsman? And out of seven brothers, who could have thought that the honor was meant for the youngest? And then by what means did he afterwards come within reach of the throne? Who dares to say that his anointing was directed by human art, skill, or prudence, and was not rather the fulfillment of a divine prophecy? In the same way, do not the predictions, though obscure, of the admission of the Gentiles into the divine covenant—since they were not fulfilled until almost two thousand years later—make it plain that Moses spoke under divine inspiration? I pass over other predictions which so plainly bear the marks of divine revelation that all men of sound mind must see that they were spoken by God. In short, his Song itself (Deut 32) is a bright mirror in which God is clearly seen.

8\. In the case of the other prophets the evidence is even clearer. I will choose only a few examples, for it would be too tedious to list them all. In his own day, when the kingdom of Judah was at peace, and even thought it had some protection laid up for it in the Chaldeans, Isaiah spoke of the destruction of the city and the captivity of the people. Suppose it were not sufficient evidence of divine inspiration to foretell, many years before, events which at the time seemed fabulous but which in the end turned out true; where shall we say the prophecies he uttered about their return came from, if not from God? He names Cyrus (Isa 45:1), by whom the Chaldeans were to be subdued and the people restored to freedom. After the prophet spoke this, more than a hundred years passed before Cyrus was born, that being about the time between the death of the one and the birth of the other. It was impossible at that time to guess that some Cyrus would arise to make war on the Babylonians, and, after subduing their powerful monarchy, put an end to the captivity of the children of Israel. Does not this simple, unadorned narrative plainly prove that what Isaiah spoke was not the conjecture of man, but the undoubted oracle of God? Again, when Jeremiah (Jer 25:11, 12), a considerable time before the people were led away, set seventy years as the period of captivity, and announced their liberation and return, must not his tongue have been guided by the Spirit of God? What effrontery it would be to deny that by these evidences the authority of the prophets is established, the very thing being fulfilled to which they appeal in support of their credibility! “Behold, the former things have happened, and now I declare new things. Before they spring forth I proclaim them to you#super[+],” (Isa 42:9). I say nothing of the agreement between Jeremiah and Ezekiel, who, living so far apart and yet prophesying at the same time, agree as completely in all they say as if each had dictated the words to the other. What shall I say of Daniel? Did he not deliver prophecies covering a future period of almost six hundred years, as if he had been writing of past events generally known?#footnote[Dan 9, etc. (Beveridge’s note.)] If the godly will duly meditate on these things, they will be well enough equipped to silence the barkings of the ungodly. The proof is too clear to be contradicted.

9\. I know what is muttered in corners by certain scoundrels when they want to show off their sharpness in attacking divine truth. They ask how we know that Moses and the prophets wrote the books which now bear their names. They even dare to question whether there ever was a Moses. If anyone were to question whether there ever was a Plato, or an Aristotle, or a Cicero, would not the rod or the whip be thought the fitting punishment for such folly? The law of Moses has been wonderfully preserved, more by divine providence than by human care. And though, through the negligence of the priests, it lay buried for a short time, yet from the time when good King Josiah found it,#footnote[2 Kgs 22:8; 2 Chr 34:15. (Beveridge’s note.)] it has continued in the hands of men, and been handed down in unbroken succession from generation to generation. Nor, indeed, when Josiah brought it out, was it a book unknown or new, but one which had always been common knowledge, and was then well remembered. The original copy had been placed in the temple, and a copy taken from it had been placed in the royal archives;#footnote[Deut 17:18, 19. (Beveridge’s note.)] the only thing that had happened was that the priests had stopped publishing the law itself in the customary way, and the people too had neglected their usual reading of it. I may add that scarcely an age passed in which its authority was not confirmed and renewed. Were the books of Moses unknown to those who had the Psalms of David in their hands? To sum up the whole in one word, it is certain beyond dispute that these writings passed down, if I may put it so, from hand to hand, being handed on in an unbroken series from the fathers, who either had heard the writers themselves speak, or learned from those who had heard them, while the memory was fresh, that they had so spoken.

10\. An objection taken from the history of the Maccabees (1 Macc. 1:57, 58) to undermine the credibility of Scripture is, on the contrary, as well fitted as anything could be to confirm it. First, however, let us clear away the gloss they put on it; having done so, we shall turn the siege engine they raise against us back on themselves. Since Antiochus ordered all the books of Scripture to be burned, they ask, where did the copies we now have come from? I in turn ask, in what workshop could they have been fabricated so quickly? It is certain that they were in existence the moment the persecution ceased, and that they were acknowledged without dispute by all the godly who had been brought up in their doctrine and knew them well. Indeed, while all the wicked insulted the Jews so wantonly, as if they had joined in a league for the purpose, not one ever dared to charge them with having brought in spurious books. Whatever they thought of the Jewish religion, they acknowledged that Moses was its founder. What, then, do those babblers do but betray their snarling insolence, in falsely claiming that books are spurious whose sacred antiquity is proved by the consent of all history? But not to waste labor in refuting such vile slanders, let us rather consider the care which the Lord took to preserve his Word, when against all hope he rescued it from the savagery of a most cruel tyrant, as from the midst of the flames. He inspired godly priests and others with such constancy that they did not hesitate to hand down this treasure to posterity, though it should cost them their lives; and he defeated the keenest search of governors and their henchmen.

Who does not recognize it as a remarkable and miraculous work of God that those sacred records, which the ungodly had persuaded themselves had utterly perished, at once returned to resume their former rights, and indeed in greater honor? For the Greek translation followed, to spread them over the whole world. Nor did the miracle appear only in this, that God rescued the tables of his covenant from the bloodthirsty edicts of Antiochus, but also in this, that they remained safe and whole amid the many disasters by which the Jewish nation was from time to time crushed, laid waste, and almost wiped out. The Hebrew language was held in no esteem, and was almost unknown; and surely, if God had not provided for religion, the language would have utterly perished. For it is obvious from the prophetic writings of that age how far the Jews, after their return from the captivity, had lost the true use of their native tongue. This is worth noting, because the comparison more clearly establishes the antiquity of the Law and the Prophets. And whom did God use to preserve the doctrine of salvation contained in the Law and the Prophets, so that Christ might be revealed in his own time? The Jews, the bitterest enemies of Christ; and so Augustine rightly calls them the librarians of the Christian Church, because they supplied us with books of which they themselves made no use.

11\. When we come to the New Testament, how solid are the pillars that support its truth! Three evangelists give their narrative in a lowly and humble style. The proud often look on this simplicity with disdain, because they do not attend to the main heads of doctrine; for from these they might easily infer that these evangelists treat of heavenly mysteries beyond the capacity of man. Those who have the least particle of decency must be ashamed of their fastidiousness when they read the first chapter of Luke. Even our Savior’s discourses, of which these three evangelists give a summary, easily exempt their writings from all contempt. John, again, thundering in majesty, strikes down more powerfully than any thunderbolt the obstinacy of those whom he does not compel to the obedience of faith. Let all those sharp-witted critics come forward whose highest pleasure is to drive reverence for Scripture out of their own and other men’s hearts; let them read the Gospel of John, and, willing or unwilling, they will find a thousand sentences that will at least rouse them from their sloth; indeed, that will burn into their consciences as with a hot iron, and check their derision. The same may be said of Peter and Paul, whose writings, though most people read them blindfold, show a heavenly majesty which in a manner binds and holds every reader fast. But one circumstance, sufficient by itself to raise their doctrine above the world, is that Matthew, who was formerly tied to his money-table, and Peter and John, who were busy with their little boats, all rough and unlearned men, had never learned in any human school what they delivered to others. Paul, moreover, who had been not only an open enemy but a cruel and bloody one, was changed into a new man, and by that sudden and unhoped-for change he shows that a heavenly power had compelled him to preach the doctrine which he once tried to destroy. Let those dogs deny that the Holy Spirit came down upon the apostles, or, if not, let them refuse to believe the history; still the very facts proclaim that the Holy Spirit must have been the teacher of those who, formerly despised among the people, all of a sudden began to speak so magnificently of heavenly mysteries.

12\. Add, moreover, that for the best of reasons the consent of the Church is not without its weight. For it is not to be counted of no importance that, from the first publication of Scripture, so many ages have steadily agreed in yielding obedience to it; and that, despite the many extraordinary attempts which Satan and the whole world have made to crush and overthrow it, or to wipe it completely from the memory of men, it has flourished like the palm tree and continued invincible. Though in old times there was scarcely a sophist or orator of any note who did not turn his powers against it, their efforts came to nothing. The powers of the earth armed themselves for its destruction, but all their attempts vanished into smoke. When it was so powerfully attacked on every side, how could it have held out if it had trusted only in human aid? Indeed, its divine origin is all the more established by the fact that, when all human wishes were against it, it advanced by its own strength. Add that it was not a single city or a single nation that agreed in receiving and embracing it. Its authority was recognized as far and as wide as the world extends, various nations who had nothing else in common entering for this purpose into a holy league. Moreover, while we ought to give the greatest weight to the agreement of minds so diverse, and in all other things so much at odds with each other—an agreement which divine providence alone could have produced—it adds no small weight to the whole when we look at the piety of those who so agree; not of all of them, indeed, but of those in whom, as in lights, God was pleased that his Church should shine.

13\. Again, with what confidence ought we to subscribe to a doctrine attested and confirmed by the blood of so many saints? Once they had embraced it, they did not hesitate to meet death in its defense boldly and fearlessly, and even with great eagerness. Since it has been handed down to us with such a pledge, which of us will not receive it with firm and unshaken conviction? It is therefore no small proof of the authority of Scripture that it was sealed with the blood of so many witnesses, especially when we consider that in bearing testimony to the faith they met death not in fanatical frenzy (as erring spirits sometimes do), but with a firm and constant, yet sober, godly zeal. There are other reasons, neither few nor weak, by which the dignity and majesty of the Scriptures may be not only proved to the godly, but also completely vindicated against the quibbles of slanderers. These, however, cannot by themselves produce a firm faith in Scripture until our heavenly Father reveals his presence in it, and so puts reverence for it beyond all dispute. Then only, therefore, does Scripture suffice to give a saving knowledge of God, when its certainty is founded on the inward persuasion of the Holy Spirit. Still, the human testimonies which serve to confirm it will not be without effect if they are used in subordination to that chief and highest proof, as secondary helps to our weakness. But it is foolish to try to prove to unbelievers that Scripture is the Word of God. It cannot be known to be this except by faith. Rightly, therefore, does Augustine remind us that every man who would have any understanding in such high matters must first have piety and peace of mind.
